// Practice quiz: Inheritance
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, and code is described in words where GIFT syntax would clash.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/inheritance-and-design/inheritance

::o1 inherited members::Class Manager extends Employee. Employee declares a public method pay() and a private field salary. Which can code inside Manager use directly by name? {
~Both pay() and salary#Not quite. salary is private, so only code inside Employee can name it.
~Neither, until Manager redeclares them#Not quite. Inherited members are available without redeclaring them.
=pay(), but not salary#Correct. A subclass inherits public and protected members; private members stay private to Employee, even though every Manager object still holds that field.
~salary, but not pay()#Not quite. It is the other way round: the public method is accessible and the private field is not.
}

::o1 single superclass::How many classes can one Java class name after extends? {
~As many as it needs, separated by commas#Not quite. That form is for implements, not extends, on a class.
~Two at most#Not quite. There is no limit of two; a class extends one class.
~None; Java has no class inheritance#Not quite. extends is how a class inherits from another class.
=Exactly one#Correct. Java classes have single inheritance; extra behaviour comes from implementing interfaces.
}

::o2 constructor order::Class C extends B, and B extends A. Each constructor prints its class name. What prints for new C()? {
~C, then B, then A#Not quite. The subclass body runs only after its superclass constructor has finished.
=A, then B, then C#Correct. Each constructor first runs its superclass constructor, so the chain completes from the top down.
~Only C#Not quite. Every superclass constructor in the chain runs, explicitly or implicitly.
~A and C, but not B#Not quite. B is part of the chain, so its constructor runs too.
}

::o2 implicit super::A subclass constructor does not call super(...) or this(...). What does Java do? {
=It inserts a call to the superclass no-argument constructor#Correct. If the superclass has no accessible no-argument constructor, the subclass does not compile.
~It skips the superclass constructor#Not quite. Some superclass constructor always runs first.
~It calls every superclass constructor#Not quite. Exactly one superclass constructor runs.
~It copies the superclass fields without running any constructor#Not quite. Fields are initialized by running a superclass constructor.
}

::o3 is-a test::Which pair is the strongest case for class B extends A? {
~A Car has an Engine#Not quite. has-a describes a part; hold the Engine as a field instead.
=A SavingsAccount is an Account and can be used wherever an Account is expected#Correct. Inheritance fits when the subclass truly is a kind of the superclass and can stand in for it.
~An Invoice uses a Printer#Not quite. uses-a suggests passing or holding a collaborator, not extending it.
~A Stack wants to reuse a few list methods#Not quite. Wanting some methods is not an is-a relationship and exposes methods that break the stack's rules.
}

::o3 substitution::Square extends Rectangle, but setting a Square's width also changes its height. Which problem does that reveal? {
=Code written for Rectangle can break when given a Square#Correct. If a subclass cannot keep the superclass's promises, the is-a relationship does not really hold.
~Square cannot inherit Rectangle's fields#Not quite. It can; the problem is behaviour, not access.
~The compiler rejects the class#Not quite. It compiles; the mismatch shows up at run time.
~Nothing, because every square is a rectangle in geometry#Not quite. A geometric fact does not guarantee that the classes behave interchangeably.
}
